  "protocol_text": "# Short documentation design probes\n\nQuestion: Do older and current documentation help a fresh model choose and explain feature ownership?\n\n## Frozen design\n\n- P1/P4: September 28 baseline archive; P2/P3: September 29 current source archive.\n- Four independent native subagents with no inherited conversation. Same model as parent; exact runtime model identifier is not exposed by spawn results. No paid external worker.\n- Initial stage: same document-library request, no docs; <=650 words and <=35 illustrative JavaScript lines.\n- Exposure: begin with material/docs/readme.md. Follow relevant links, at most six textual files and 64,000 characters total. No runtime, other folders, external tools or conversations. <=650 words and <=35 JavaScript lines, log exact files read, preserve initial answer.\n- Neutral interview after revised design: choices, cited passages, lifetime walkthrough, perceived cost. No Application hint until those answers are saved.\n- Final counterfactual: ask explicitly about Application, retained root View, and plain controller; collect tradeoffs without feedback.\n\n## Assessment declared before documentation stages\n\nRecord whether initial readiness uses Application/prepareStart; retained requests and data survive replaceable presentation; shared state uses observable delivery; Regions/Views own child presentation; cleanup separates feature stop from editor replacement; snippets expose concrete wiring versus leave central helpers unspecified. Mark omitted or unverified behavior rather than assume implementation correctness. Architectural naming alone is insufficient. Initial stage can name uncertain APIs without penalty for documentation ignorance.\n\n## Limits\n\nSmall sample, same model family, design-only and architecture-salient request; not natural implementation or Claude/Stringent replication. Same-session revision is anchored on the initial answer. Docs differ in volume, organization, and runtime-era contracts. Read limits exclude some material. Filesystem isolation is by instruction, not sandbox. Self-reports are hypotheses; observable design changes are stronger evidence. This document-library task is now exposed diagnostic material, not a holdout.\n",
  "prompts": {
    "initial": "You are an independent participant in a short design exercise. Do not consult other agents, conversation history, repository files, memory, web, or documentation yet. No implementation or tests. You may use a tool only to write your answer to PARTICIPANT/initial.md. Task: Design a Marionette v5 document-library feature. It loads documents through libraryApi.list(), lets the user open an editor for a document title, and saves through libraryApi.saveTitle(id, title). A filter changes the list without disturbing the open editor's text or focus. Closing/replacing the editor must not cancel a pending save; reopening shows the current draft and save status. A deliberate refresh retains the last successful data while loading. Stopping the whole feature makes its UI and pending completions inactive. Existing page navigation remains alive. Give a concise design and at most 35 lines of illustrative JavaScript showing the core wiring. Identify who owns data, requests, presentation, and cleanup. Mark uncertain API names instead of assuming their validity. Limit the entire answer to 650 words. Preserve this initial answer in the named file and return a short summary. Do not inspect the surrounding workspace. Await a follow-up after finishing.",
    "documentation": "Now revise the same design using only the supplied version-matched documentation under PARTICIPANT/material. Start at material/docs/readme.md and choose the relevant links yourself. Read at most six textual files and 64,000 total characters of file contents; choose excerpts if needed. Do not read outside your own participant directory, runtime source, other agents, web, memory, or repo. Documentation examples are available if linked, within the same reading limit. Keep initial.md unchanged. Save revised.md with <=650 words and <=35 illustrative JavaScript lines. Show concrete public API wiring where possible; identify pseudocode/unverified behavior. Append a Reading log outside the word limit listing exact relative file paths, ranges/characters read, and which source sections support the changes. Record deliberate decisions to retain the initial design. Do not run tests or implement an application. Return a short summary and await another follow-up.",
    "interview": "Do not revise either design or read more documentation. Based only on your recorded designs and reading, answer in <=450 words and save interview.md in your participant directory. Give a brief design rationale, not hidden chain-of-thought. (1) What determined your chosen owners? (2) Which exact passage most influenced a concrete change? Cite the supplied relative path and section. If nothing changed, say so. (3) Walk through editor closure during a pending save and later feature stop: what remains alive, what mutates, and what is destroyed? (4) Which central behavior did your short code leave as a helper or prose, and is that because of space, uncertainty, or an API gap? (5) What alternatives did you consider and what would make you choose one? (6) Which documentation statement was hardest to apply? Distinguish recollection from speculation. Do not claim this explains another model's behavior. Return a brief summary and await a follow-up.\n",
    "counterfactual": "Do not change your earlier answers or read more files. Save counterfactual.md, <=300 words. Explicit counterfactual: would a retained root View, a plain controller, or Marionette Application best implement this same task, and why? What does Application remove from your implementation, and what custom request/state logic remains? Did the task asking you to identify owners, plus the code-length limit, make this design exercise easier or different from implementing a product ticket? Identify a concrete change in requirements that would make a View sufficient. Report concise design reasons, not private reasoning. This is a prompted comparison and must not be presented as your spontaneous initial choice.\n"
  },
